  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nVery warm to hot temperatures with very little pattern change \npersists through the rest of the week into early next weekend. \nWhile heat advisory criteria is not expected to be met during the \ncontinued stretch of summer weather as we head into September, \neveryone is still encouraged to take proper heat safety \nprecautions. The area will remain on the western side of mid to \nupper ridging, underneath passing disturbances providing lift and \nmoisture convergence that interact with persistent near surface \nhumid, upslope southeast winds. This keeps low (20% to 30%) \nshower/storm chances in the forecast each afternoon/evening, with \nhighest chances for the western higher terrain. At this time, no \nmajor flooding or severe weather is expected with storms, and main\nrisks in strongest storms look to remain damaging winds and \npatchy blowing dust, frequent lightning, small hail, and brief \nheavy rainfall. Despite September 1st being the climatological \nstart of autumn, no major cooling pattern or even a more zonal mid\nto upper air pattern is apparent through next weekend in models. \nAn expansive mid to upper ridge in fact looks to build across the \ncentral CONUS through at least next weekend. Details thereafter \nremain murky. We will continue to keep an eye on both \ndeterministic and ensemble forecasts of the mid to upper air \npattern and the tropics-mid latitude upper air wave interactions \nto see if there is any sign of ridging breaking down beyond the \nfirst week of September.",
